//! Key agreement (ECDH) and ECDSA signatures on the NIST P-384 curve (secp384r1).
//!
//! # When to use
//! Use [`P384`] for an ECDH key exchange where a protocol needs P-384, such as a TLS 1.3 key share
//! `secp384r1`, and to sign with ECDSA, as in `ES384` JSON Web Tokens or Node.js `crypto.sign`. Use
//! [`EcdsaP384Sha384`] to verify a DER signature with SHA-384, for example in a certificate chain,
//! and [`EcdsaP384`] to verify with another hash or in the fixed 96-byte form.
//!
//! # Details
//! Public keys are 97 bytes in uncompressed SEC1 form: `0x04`, then the X and Y coordinates, each
//! 48 bytes big-endian. [`P384.compressPublicKey`] and [`P384.decompressPublicKey`] convert to and
//! from the 49-byte compressed form. Secrets are 48 bytes big-endian. [`P384.agree`] returns the X
//! coordinate of the shared point and consumes the key. [`P384.sign`] returns `r` and `s`, each 48
//! bytes, with the deterministic nonce of RFC 6979. [`EcdsaP384.toDer`] and [`EcdsaP384.fromDer`]
//! convert signatures to and from DER. Nothing allocates.
//!
//! # Errors
//! - [`P384Error`] for a secret or a peer public key that is not valid.
//! - [`P384SignatureError`] for a key or signature that is not valid, or that does not match.
//!
//! # Gotchas
//! A valid signature proves only that the holder of the private key signed the message. It does not
//! prove that you can trust the key. To check a certificate chain, use the `silk.tls.trust_*`
//! modules. The key exchange does not authenticate the peer. Pass the shared secret through
//! `silk.crypto.hkdf` before you use it as a key. The secret is not erased when it is dropped.
//!
//! # Examples
//! ## Verify a signature
//! ```silk
//! import silk.crypto.p384 { EcdsaP384Sha384, P384SignatureError }
//! import silk.core.result { Result }
//!
//! pub fn main() -> i32 {
//!   // A public key and a signature of "hello" that another program made.
//!   let publicKey: [u8; 97] = [
//!     0x04,
//!     0xe8,
//!     0x6b,
//!     0x99,
//!     0x5e,
//!     0x35,
//!     0x0e,
//!     0x4d,
//!     0x31,
//!     0x2a,
//!     0x95,
//!     0xa6,
//!     0xb7,
//!     0xd5,
//!     0xd8,
//!     0xcd,
//!     0xcd,
//!     0xb4,
//!     0x68,
//!     0x2f,
